# Normalise line endings in the repository, and leave the working tree alone.
#
# `* text=auto` makes git normalise to LF in the index and check out with the
# platform's own convention; `eol=lf` overrides that and checks out LF everywhere.
# The second one is the deliberate choice here, for three reasons:
#
#   - The repository has a Windows CI leg, and a maintainer on Windows. With
#     `text=auto` alone, the same file has different bytes on disk depending on
#     who checked it out, and a diff that shows every line as changed is the
#     symptom.
#   - `src/index.js` is a `bin`, with a shebang. A CRLF shebang is `/usr/bin/env
#     node\r` on Linux and macOS: "bad interpreter", and the installed package
#     does not start.
#   - Every generated artefact — the tarball, a lockfile diff, a `.tgz` checksum —
#     is byte-sensitive, and CRLF is the kind of difference that is invisible in
#     review and visible in a checksum.
#
# WHY THE BINARY EXCEPTIONS ARE HERE
# ----------------------------------
# The rule is `text` and not `-text`, so git decides by inspection. That is right
# for a repository with no binary files, and the two entries below are not
# belt-and-braces: they are there so that a `.tgz` or a `.node` added later is not
# line-ending-damaged on its way into the index. A tarball mended by git is a
# tarball npm cannot install.
#
# THIS IS NOT NORMALISED AT COMMIT TIME, AND THAT IS THE POINT
# -----------------------------------------------------------
# Every tracked file in the index is LF today. That is not an accident of this
# file: it is what `text` means, and it is why `git add --renormalize .` has
# nothing left to do here. An earlier revision of this comment claimed five
# files were still CRLF in the index and told the next maintainer to run that
# command; the index had no CRLF at all, so the advice was a chore with no effect
# dressed up as a fix.
#
# The gap is not in the index, it is between the index and `npm publish`.
# `text=auto` makes git convert CRLF to LF *when it compares the working tree to
# the index*, so a file with CRLF on disk reports as clean in `git status`.
# `npm publish` does not consult the index at all: it packs the working-tree
# bytes. anydb-mcp@3.0.1 was published that way, and 3.0.2 shipped
# `src/core/safety.js` with 694 CRLF — from a tree that git considered clean and
# that no diff would show.
#
# So the policy here is necessary and not sufficient. `scripts/verify-package.mjs`
# closes the gap by reading the packed tarball rather than the repository, which
# is the only one of the three views that a consumer can observe.
#
# A local `core.autocrlf=true` fights `eol=lf` and leaves `git status` reporting
# files as modified that `git diff` then shows as identical. It is noise, not a
# change; `git add` stages nothing. Setting `core.autocrlf=false` for this
# repository silences it, but nothing depends on it being set either way.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.js   text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.cjs  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Not text, and never to be line-ending-processed.
*.tgz    binary
*.node   binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.zip    binary
*.gz     binary

# Lockfiles and tarballs are generated and compared byte for byte.
package-lock.json text eol=lf
